STRUCTURE OF THE credits.csv FILE

Three columns: cast, crew, id.
One row per movie.

- id
	Identifier of the movie on TMDB, the same as movies_metadata.id (e.g. 862 = Toy Story)

- cast
	List of the movie's actors. Each actor is an object with 8 fields:

    {'cast_id': 14, 'character': 'Woody (voice)', 'credit_id': '52fe4284c3a36847f8024f95', 'gender': 2,
     'id': 31, 'name': 'Tom Hanks', 'order': 0, 'profile_path': '/pQFoyx7rp09CJTAb932F2g8Nlho.jpg'}

	cast_id       number of the actor within the movie
	character     character played
	credit_id     unique code of this "participation" (person + movie + role)
	gender        gender of the person: 0 = not specified, 1 = female, 2 = male (TMDB encoding)
	id            identifier of the PERSON on TMDB (not of the movie!)
	name          name of the person
	order         position in the credits: 0 = first actor, 1 = second, ...
	profile_path  photo of the person on the TMDB website (None if missing)

- crew
	List of the people who worked on the movie behind the camera. Each person is an object with 7 fields:

    {'credit_id': '52fe4284c3a36847f8024f49', 'department': 'Directing', 'gender': 2, 'id': 7879,
     'job': 'Director', 'name': 'John Lasseter', 'profile_path': '/7EdqiNbr4FRjIhKHyPPdFfEEEFG.jpg'}

	department    department (Directing, Writing, Production, Sound, ...)
	job           specific role (Director, Screenplay, Producer, ...)
	credit_id, gender, id, name, profile_path   as in the cast

    As in the other files, the lists are written in Python format (single quotes): they are read with ast.literal_eval.
    Warning: inside cast and crew "id" is the person, while the id column of the row is the movie


FINDINGS FROM THE EDA SCRIPTS


- Structure (section 1):
	45,476 rows, all with exactly 3 fields: no broken rows.
	All ids are numeric, cast and crew can always be read and are always lists.
	All the cast objects have the same 8 fields, all the crew objects the same 7,
	always with the same type. The only field that can be missing is profile_path (None).

- Duplicated IDs (section 2):
	43 ids appear more than once, for a total of 87 rows (42 ids twice, 1 id three times).
	Keeping one copy per id removes 44 rows.

	36 ids have identical copies.

	The other 7 look different, but the script shows that they have the same credit_ids and no field
	with a different value: only the ORDER of the people in the crew list changes.
	So in practice they are all copies of the same data: keeping one is enough.

	29 of these ids are the same duplicates as in movies_metadata, 14 are duplicated only here.

- Movies without cast or without crew (section 3):
	empty cast 2,414 movies (5.3%), empty crew 771 (1.7%), both empty 421 (0.9%).
	As for keywords, it depends on how well known the movie is:

	votes     empty cast    empty crew
	0         21.3%         9.6%
	1-9        7.9%         2.3%
	10-99      1.4%         0.3%
	100-999    0.2%         0.0%
	1000+      0.0%         0.0%

- Missing data inside the lists (sections 4 and 5):
	empty character in 25,767 cast rows (4.6%).
	gender not specified (0): 39.8% of the cast and 58.6% of the crew.
	-> any query on gender works only on part of the people.
	missing profile_path: 30.9% of the cast, 79.5% of the crew (not relevant for the queries).

- Spaces in names (section 6):
	498 names have spaces at the start or at the end (99 with a tab, 1 with \xa0), e.g. 'Stuart Reynolds '.
	72 of these, once the spaces are removed, match the name of another person (different id).
	From the data it is impossible to tell whether they are the same person registered twice or two namesakes.


